!! Process bridge — a child process whose output reaches AllSpeak handlers.
!!
!! The shape Scenario Studio needs: a window whose Run button starts a Python child, whose stdout and stderr lines reach handlers while the AllSpeak flow is parked in its wait loop, and whose exit code — or terminating signal — is reported back. One run is cancelled through the child's stdin, and one is stopped with `stop Runner`. Nothing polls the child.
!!
!! Run it from this directory, either in a window or headless:
!!
!!     allspeak bridge.allspeak
!!     QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen python3 ../../tools/process-check.py
script ProcessBridge

use graphics
use process

window Win
layout Root
layout Panes
layout Left
layout Right
label Heading
label Status
table Steps
logpane Log
process Runner
variable Stage
variable Rows
variable Lines
variable Warnings
variable FirstCode
variable SecondCode
variable ThirdSignal
!! @hash f773a186
!! @verified
!!!

!! Build the window, seed the summary table, and start the first run.
!!
!! The layout is three regions — a heading, two panes and a status line — and the panes hold the step table and the log. The rows are seeded with `set rows of`, which takes an array of arrays; each handler narrows one row with `set cell` and tints it, which is the studio's own update pattern.
!!
!! The process is started after its handlers are registered, and the flow then parks in `Idle` rather than ending: an event arrives as an intent and the runtime drains it on the Qt thread, so nothing here has to poll.
Setup: ! §1
    init graphics
    create Win title `Process bridge` size 960 640
    create Root type QVBoxLayout
    create Heading text `Ten lines, a cancelled run, then a terminated one` expand
    create Panes type QHBoxLayout
    create Left type QVBoxLayout
    create Right type QVBoxLayout
    create Steps
    create Log
    create Status text `starting`
    set columns of Steps to `["#","Run","Result"]`
    put `[["1","first run","waiting"],["2","cancel run","waiting"],["3","terminate","waiting"],["4","verdict","waiting"]]` into Rows
    set rows of Steps to Rows
    set column widths of Steps to `[40,140,280]`
    set stretch column of Steps to 2
    set cell tooltip Steps 3 1 to `the verdict row`
    set cell alignment Steps 3 0 to center
    add Heading to Root
    add Panes to Root
    add Left to Panes
    add Right to Panes
    add Steps to Left
    add Log to Right
    add Status to Root
    set the layout of Win to Root
    on select Steps go to StepSelected
    on click Steps go to StepClicked
    on line Runner go to RunnerLine
    on stderr line Runner go to RunnerError
    on exit Runner go to RunnerDone
    show Win
    put 0 into Lines
    put 0 into Warnings
    put 0 into FirstCode
    put 0 into SecondCode
    put 0 into ThirdSignal
    set Stage to `first`
    start Runner command `python3 linewriter.py ten`
Idle:
    wait 1 tick
    go to Idle
!! @hash 0d018158
!! @verified
!!!

!! A stdout line, counted and shown.
!!
!! The count is what the verdict is made of, so it is kept in a variable rather than read back out of the pane: a log line arrives one at a time, and the handler must leave nothing to the widget's own state. Only the first run's lines are counted — the two later runs exist to be cancelled and stopped, not to be measured.
RunnerLine: ! §2
    if Stage is `first`
    begin
        add 1 to Lines
    end
    append the line of Runner to Log
    return
!! @hash 255c29a0
!! @verified
!!!

!! A stderr line, in red.
!!
!! stderr is delivered as its own event, which is why it has its own handler: a child that reports a problem on stderr must not have that line counted as output, and a reader must be able to see it is a problem.
RunnerError: ! §3
    add 1 to Warnings
    append the stderr line of Runner to Log with color `#B00020`
    return
!! @hash eed832eb
!! @verified
!!!

!! When a run ends: record how, then start or finish the next run.
!!
!! The first exit starts the cancel run and writes the cancel line to its stdin straight away — the pipe buffers it, so no wait is needed. The second exit starts a third run and stops it at once, which is the `stop Runner` route and ends in a terminating signal rather than an exit code. The third exit checks what all three runs did, writes the verdict row, and closes the window: the runtime stops any child still running, and the Qt loop ends itself.
RunnerDone: ! §4
    if Stage is `first`
    begin
        put the exit code of Runner into FirstCode
        set cell Steps 0 2 to `exit code ` cat FirstCode
        set cell colour Steps 0 2 to `#1E6B2F` `#E4F2E8`
        set the text of Status to `first run finished; cancelling a second`
        set Stage to `cancel`
        start Runner command `python3 linewriter.py wait`
        write `{"command":"cancel"}` to Runner
        return
    end
    if Stage is `cancel`
    begin
        put the exit code of Runner into SecondCode
        set cell Steps 1 2 to `exit code ` cat SecondCode
        set cell colour Steps 1 2 to `#8A5A00` `#F6EBD9`
        set the text of Status to `cancel run finished; stopping a third`
        set Stage to `stop`
        start Runner command `python3 linewriter.py wait`
        stop Runner
        return
    end
    put the signal of Runner into ThirdSignal
    set cell Steps 2 2 to `signal ` cat ThirdSignal
    set cell colour Steps 2 2 to `#8A5A00` `#F6EBD9`
    check Lines is 10
    check Warnings is 1
    check FirstCode is 0
    check SecondCode is 3
    check ThirdSignal is 15
    check the row count of Steps is 4
    check the cell 0 2 of Steps is `exit code 0`
    check the position of `cancelled` in the text of Log is greater than 0
    if Lines is 10 and FirstCode is 0 and SecondCode is 3 and ThirdSignal is 15
    begin
        set cell Steps 3 2 to `all checks passed`
        set cell colour Steps 3 2 to `#1E6B2F` `#E4F2E8`
        set the text of Status to `all checks passed`
    end
    else begin
        set cell Steps 3 2 to `failed`
        set cell colour Steps 3 2 to `#B00020` `#F8E4E4`
        set the text of Status to `failed`
    end
    close Win
    return
!! @hash 718640c4
!! @verified
!!!

!! Selecting a row reports it, so the selection route is exercised.
!!
!! The studio's inspector reads the selected row; this is the smallest form of that: the status line says which row the pointer is over, and the handler ends with `return` because a named event handler has nothing to go back to.
StepSelected: ! §5
    set the text of Status to `selected row ` cat the selected row of Steps
    return
!! @hash 17f82214
!! @verified
!!!

!! A click reports the cell under the pointer.
!!
!! Row selection and cell clicking are different questions — the studio selects a row to inspect it, and clicks a cell to edit it — so they have different handlers and different values. `the clicked row` and `the clicked column` name the cell, not the selected row.
StepClicked: ! §6
    set the text of Status to `clicked row ` cat the clicked row of Steps cat ` column ` cat the clicked column of Steps
    return
!! @hash f6feaefc
!! @verified
!!!
